course: dictionary
lesson: 6324
title: Motivation
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: «Seeing your coins pile up in a see-through jar helps keep you going; this push to keep going is called motivation.» The sentence tells you what motivation is: the push that keeps you going, even when a task is long or hard.
explain: Motivation is a word you will meet at school, in sport and in stories. Coaches talk about it, scientists ask why animals behave as they do, and writers show a character's reasons for acting. When a text says «lack of motivation», it means there is very little wish or energy to do something.
explain: Look at the clues around the word. In the coin jar sentence, watching the coins pile up is what keeps you going, so motivation is the drive you get from seeing your progress. Swap your meaning into the sentence. If it still makes sense, you have got it.
fact: Motivation means the wish or energy to do something and keep trying.
fact: Motivation can also mean a reason for doing something.
fact: In formal writing, a motivation can be something that gives a reason or encourages action.
fact: When you mean the feeling itself, motivation is uncountable: say «lots of motivation», not «a motivation».
fact: Useful phrases include «lose motivation», «a strong motivation» and «the motivation behind» something.
exercise: «Seeing your coins pile up helps keep you going; this push is called motivation.» What is motivation? | A jar with a lid | A lazy feeling | A kind of coin | *The wish or energy to keep trying
exercise: Ella says, «I have lost motivation for piano this term.» What does lost motivation suggest? | She has found a new piano | She practises every day with great energy | *She has stopped feeling like practising | She has bought a bigger piano
exercise: In a story, the author explains the motivation behind the fox's actions. What is a motivation here? | A loud noise in the woods | A type of fox | The last page of the story | *The reason for doing something
test: Which phrase means someone has very little wish to try? | The motivation behind | A strong motivation | A jar of coins | *Lack of motivation
test: Which sentence uses motivation correctly? | *Where's your motivation today? | Where's your a motivation today? | Where's your motivations today?
exam: In «this push to keep going is called motivation», what does motivation mean? | A slow walk to school | The coins in the jar | A broken jar | *The push that keeps you going
